	abstract = {Reef fish communities at Gray's Reef National Marine Sanctuary, Georgia,{\textbackslash}ndiffered over five different habitat types. Numbers of species and{\textbackslash}noverall densities were highest on ledge habitat, intermediate on{\textbackslash}nlive-bottom (three categories of low relief less then15 cm rock outcroppings{\textbackslash}ncovered by algae and macrofauna), and lowest over sand. On average,{\textbackslash}nabundance over ledges exceeded that over sand bottoms by a factor{\textbackslash}nof 50. Generally, community composition at sites over ledges and{\textbackslash}ndense live-bottom areas was similar and distinct from sites found{\textbackslash}nover sparse live-bottom and sand. Many species were found in more{\textbackslash}nthan one habitat and few individual species could be considered indicators{\textbackslash}nof a single habitat type. A nondestructive, repeatable procedure{\textbackslash}nof randomly dispersed video transects was devised for assessing diurnally{\textbackslash}nactive fishes.},
